      },
      "summary": "The changed tests cover all four acceptance criteria: separate log and trace exporter registration checks, redaction of properties, scopes, activity tags and event attributes, and regression checks for the filter and listed sensitive names. I found no concrete test defect within the review scope.",
      "findingList": []
    },
    {
      "agentId": "security-reviewer",
      "reviewType": "security",
      "harness": "codex",
      "harnessVersion": "0.162.0",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "approved": false,
      "findings": {
        "critical": 0,
        "major": 1,
        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "Most listed property names and credential shapes are redacted before logging or trace export. The JWT matcher can miss valid tokens whose encoded JSON header does not start with `eyJ`.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Diagnostics/RedactionFilter.cs",
          "line": 24,
          "message": "The JWT matcher assumes the encoded header starts with `eyJ`, so valid JWTs with JSON whitespace in the header can bypass redaction (OWASP A09:2025).",
          "confidence": 0.86,
          "category": "security",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "Log properties, scopes, and trace attributes pass string values through ScrubText, where Jwt() only matches tokens whose first segment starts with `eyJ`.",
            "failureScenario": "A caller logs a compact JWT whose valid JSON header has leading whitespace, such as a base64url-encoded ` {\"alg\":\"HS256\"}` header. Its first segment does not start with `eyJ`, so the token can reach the log provider or trace exporter unchanged, despite the criterion requiring three-part base64url JWTs to be redacted. OWASP A09:2025."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
